%N A400324 Irregular triangle read by rows, where row n gives the lengths of consecutive same species move blocks in the solution of the Frogs and Toads interchange puzzle with n frogs and n toads.
%C A400324 For each n>=1, consider the solution of the Frogs and Toads interchange puzzle with n frogs initially on the left, n toads initially on the right, and one empty position between the two groups. Group consecutive moves made by pieces of the same species into maximal blocks. The length of these blocks form row n. Row n has length 2*n + 1 and is 1,2,..., n-1, n, n, n, n-1, ...,2,1. The sum of row n is n*(n+2).
%D A400324 Derek Holton, The frog problem: more than you might want to know, The Mathematical Gazette, Vol. 93, No. 528 (2009), pp. 398-403.
%D A400324 Paul M. E. Shutler, Yet more frogs, International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology, Vol. 42, no. 4 (2011), pp. 524-533.
%H A400324 Jaap Scherphuis, <a href="https://www.jaapsch.net/puzzles/froghop.htm">Frog Hop Puzzle</a>, Jaap's puzzle page.
%F A400324 T(n,k) = min(k, 2*n + 2 - k, n), with 1 <= k <= 2*n + 1.
%e A400324 For n = 2, the puzzle's solution consists of five consecutive same-species move blocks, of lengths 1,2,2,2,1. Thus, row T(2,k) = (1,2,2,2,1). The trapezoidal shape reads as follows:
%e A400324           1  1  1
%e A400324        1  2  2  2  1
%e A400324     1  2  3  3  3  2  1
%e A400324  1  2  3  4  4  4  3  2  1
%t A400324 StringRiffle[ToString /@ Flatten[Table[Join[Range[n], {n, n}, Reverse[Range[n - 1]]],{n, 1, 20}]],","]
%Y A400324 Cf. A005563.
%K A400324 nonn,tabf,new
%O A400324 1,5
%A A400324 _Dario T. de Castro_, Sep 23 2026
